[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] There exists $c_\star\in(0,\infty)$ such that the quantity $\epsilon_{n,t,u}$ defined for integer $n\ge1$ and real $t>0$ and~$u>0$ by
\begin{equation}

P^\varrho \Bigl(\,\max_{x\in\BbbL_n} \sqrt{L_t(x)} - \sqrt{t} - a_n(t) > u \Bigr)
= c_\star \bigl(1+\epsilon_{n,t,u}\bigr) u\texte^{-2u\slb} 
\end{equation}
obeys
\begin{equation}

\lim_{m\to\infty}\sup_{t,u\ge m} \limsup_{n\to\infty}\,\bigl|\,\epsilon_{n,t,u}\bigr|=0.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 47 of arXiv:2608.27307, '2608.27307'] Under Assumption~\ref{ass:basic}, for all sufficiently large $N$, if $z,\zeta\in\C^+$ satisfy
        \begin{align}
                z=\Phi_t(\zeta), 
        \end{align}
        then
        \begin{align}
                \Pi(z)= \begin{pmatrix}a_t(\zeta)I_M & 0   \\
               0             & I_N\end{pmatrix} \Pi_0(\zeta) \begin{pmatrix}I_M & 0                  \\
               0   & a_t(\zeta)^{-1}I_N\end{pmatrix}. 
        \end{align}
        In particular,
        \begin{align}
                m(z)=a_t(\zeta)^{-1}m_0(\zeta), \qquad g(z)=a_t(\zeta)(g_0(\zeta)+th_0(\zeta)). 
        \end{align}
        Hence $m_{\rm fc}=m$, and the rectangular free convolution of $\bnu_0$ with the Mar\v{c}enko--Pastur law at time $t$ and aspect ratio $c_N$ coincides with $\bnu$.

