[Thm 2 of arXiv:2604.07370, 'Probabilistic Weyl law for twisted Toeplitz matrices with rough symbols'] Let $p\in \mathscr{C}^{0,\varrho}_{\mathrm{pw}}S_d$ and $\mathscr{U}_p$ be given by \eqref{Ensemble des singularités}. Assume that $p$ satisfies Assumptions \ref{Hypothèse théorème final - volume singularités} and \ref{Hypothèse théorème final - volume préimage}. Let $Q_N$ be an $N^d\times N^d$ random matrix satisfying Assumption \ref{Hypothèse théorème final - matrice aléatoire} for some $\kappa_3>0$. Then, for every $\delta_0>0$, setting 
    \begin{equation}
        \delta:=N^{-(\kappa_3+\delta_0)},

    \end{equation}
     the empirical spectral measure 
     \begin{equation}
        \mu_{N}:=\frac{1}{N^d}\sum_{\lambda\in \sigma(M_N(p)+\delta Q_N)}\delta_{\lambda}

    \end{equation}
     of $M_N(p)+\delta Q_N$ converges weakly in probability to the push-forward $p_*\mathrm{L}$ of the normalized Lebesgue measure on $[0,1]^d_x\times \T_\xi^{d}$ by $p$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition cocycle of arXiv:2507.08757, 'Strong existence and uniqueness of the tilt-indexed Busemann process in the planar corner '] A function $A:\mathbb{Z}^2\times\mathbb{Z}^2\to\mathbb{R}$ is a \emph{cocycle} if 
\begin{equation}\begin{aligned}
A(x,y)+A(y,z)=A(y,z)\quad\text{for all }x,y,z\in\mathbb{Z}^2.
\end{aligned}\qedhere\end{equation}

[Lemma 12 (Collision-null diffuse basis) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] The family in \eqref{primitive:eq:spatial-replicas} satisfies:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\roman*)}]
 \item
 \begin{equation}
  s!\ip{u_{N,s,\alpha}}{u_{N,s,\beta}}
  =\1_{\{\alpha=\beta\}};

 \end{equation}
 \item for every $1\le r<s$,
 \begin{equation}
  \norm{u_{N,s,\alpha}\otimes_ru_{N,s,\beta}}
  \le \frac{C_{s,r}}{\sqrt N}
  \1_{\{\alpha=\beta\}};

 \end{equation}
 \item every $u_{N,s,\alpha}$ vanishes whenever two of its spatial
 variables are at distance less than $1$.
\end{enumerate}

[Lemma 12 (Choice of cocharacter) of arXiv:2609.03954, 'Galois representations ramified at one prime via relative deformation theory'] Let $\cE\subset\Z$ be finite. There exists a cocharacter
$\lambda\in X_*(\TT)$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\arabic*)}]
\item $\langle\alpha,\lambda\rangle$ is odd for every
      $\alpha\in\Delta$;
\item $\langle\beta,\lambda\rangle\notin\cE$ for every
      $\beta\in\Phi$;
\item the integers $\langle\alpha,\lambda\rangle$, for
      $\alpha\in\mathcal B$, are pairwise distinct;
\item if $\dim Z(\GG)=1$, the image
      $\lambda^{\mathrm{ab}}\in X_*(\GG/\Gder)$ is nonzero.
\end{enumerate}

[Theorem thm:ZIMsurvivalZd1 of arXiv:2607.29409, 'The Zombie Infection Model'] If $\lambda<1.25$, then $\sup_{\Delta \Subset {\mathbb{Z}}^2}\phi(\lambda, {\mathbb{Z}}^{2}, \Delta
		)=0$ and, if $\lambda>6.92$, then $\inf_{\Delta \Subset {\mathbb{Z}}^2}\phi(\lambda, {\mathbb{Z}}
		^{2}, \Delta)>0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Defin Modified admissibility of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] For $d\geq1$ and $s\in\{0,1,\dots,d-1\}$, we call $(d,s)$-\textit{admissible} the $(d,s)$-non-degenerate weight pairs $({\mathbf{a}},{\mathbf{b}})$ for which the random compositions $(C_{nd+s})_{n\geq 0}$  with distributions $(\PComp{{\mathbf{a}},{\mathbf{b}}}{nd+s})_{n\geq 0}$ can be coupled in such a way that
	\begin{align*}
	C_s\covered[d] C_{d+s}\covered[d] C_{2d+s}\covered[d] \dots\covered[d] C_{nd+s}\covered[d] C_{(n+1)d+s}\covered[d]\dots.
	\end{align*}

[Proposition 15 of arXiv:2511.12623, 'The eigenvector bead process'] Fix \(E\in(-2,2)\) and, for each \(N\), let \(i_N(E):=\min\{k:\,\lambda_k^{(N)}\ge E\sqrt{N}\}\).
For fixed offsets \(u\in\mathbb{Z}\), set \(i:=i_N(E)+u\).
Assume that the random family \(\bigl(\mu_{j}^{(N)},\,g_{j}^{(N)}\bigr)_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\) converges almost surely, as \(N\to\infty\), to \(\bigl(\mu_{j}^{(0)},\,g_{j}^{(0)}\bigr)_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\).
Then, almost surely,
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to\infty}
\left(\frac{\Delta_{i,i}^{(N)}}{\upsilon_{i}^{(N)}}\right)^{2}
=
\bigl(\mu_i^{(1)}-\mu_i^{(0)}\bigr)^{2}
\sum_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}
\left(\frac{|g_j^{(0)}|}{\mu_i^{(1)}-\mu_j^{(0)}}\right)^{2},
\end{equation}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:as-convergence}, the variables \(\{g_j^{(0)}\}_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\) are independent Gaussian random variables (independent of \(\{\mu_j^{(0)}\}_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\)). \(\mu_i^{(1)}\) denotes the \(i\)-th solution \(z\) of the secular equation
\(
S_{\mu^{(0)},\,(g^{(0)})^{2}}(z)\;=\;-\frac{E}{2\sqrt{4-E^{2}}}\,.
\) (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition of arXiv:2512.06640, 'Existence and sharpness of the phase transition for the frog model on transitive graphs'] A graph $G=(V,E)$ satisfies the \textbf{volume doubling} condition if there exists $C > 0$ such that for all $x\in V$ and $n\geq1:$
\begin{equation}
g_{x}(2n) \leq C g_{x}(n) \, .
\end{equation}

