[Corollary 7 of arXiv:2609.01460, 'On cost-induced Santal\'o-type inequalities \\ in Polish measure spaces'] Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $\R^n$ that is log-concave with respect to $\gamma_n$. Then, for any pair of measurable functions $f, g:\R^n\to \R_+$ and any measurable, non-decreasing function $W: \R_+ \to (0, \infty)$ satisfying the pointwise relation
\begin{equation}

f(x)^{\frac{1}{2}} g(y)^{\frac{1}{2}} \leq W\left(\frac{|x-y|}{2}\right) \quad \text{for all } x, y \in \R^n,
\end{equation}
one has the functional inequality
\begin{equation}

\left( \int_{\R^n} f(x) \, d\mu(x) \right)^{\frac{1}{2}} \left( \int_{\R^n} g(y) \, d\mu(y) \right)^{\frac{1}{2}} \leq  \int_{\R} W(|r|) \, d\gamma(r).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 22 of arXiv:2605.30030, 'Critical exponents for planar random-cluster model with cluster-weight~$q=4$'] For every~$x,y \in \bbR^2$ and~$\eps \geq \delta > 0$ satisfying~$|x|/16 \geq |y| \geq 4\delta$,
    \begin{align}

    \frac{\widetilde\Delta^\delta(x,y,\eps)}{\pi_1^\delta(\eps,|y|)^4}\asymp \Delta^\delta(|y|,|x|)^2+o_{\eps,\delta}(1)
    \end{align}
    where~$o_{\eps,\delta}(1)$ is a quantity that satisfies~$\lim_{\eps\to0}\limsup_{\delta\to0} \Ll|o_{\eps,\delta}(1)\Rr|=0$. 
	The constants in~$\asymp$ are uniform in~$x$,~$y$,~$\delta$ and~$\eps$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 15 of arXiv:2609.01346, '2609.01346'] For every fixed pair $(n,k)$ satisfying \eqref{eq:nk-range} and every
$\delta_0\in(0,\delta_*)$, there is $C_x=C_x(n,k,\delta_0)$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{0<s<s_1(\delta)}x_\delta(s)\leq C_x
 \quad\text{for all }\delta\in\sS\cap[\delta_0,\delta_*).
\end{equation}

[Proposition e:QalphaY of arXiv:2607.10708, 'A class of Markov processes with resetting and applications to cybersecurity'] Let $X=(N,\Lambda,C)$  be a self-exciting PDMP with resetting and $Y=(N,\Lambda)$. The generator of  $Y$ is given by

\begin{equation} 
  \begin{split}
    Q^\alpha f(n, \lambda)
    =& -\lambda f(n, \lambda) + \alpha(\beta-\lambda)f_\lambda (n, \lambda)\\
    &+\lambda \int\mathds{1}_{\{u < A-\lambda\}} f(n+1,u+\lambda) \,dG_{\ell}(n+1, u)\\
    &+\lambda f(0,{\lambda^o}) \bar{G}_{\ell}(n+1, (A-\lambda) \textrm{-}),
  \end{split}

\end{equation}
  where  $G_{\ell}(n, \cdot)$ is the distribution function defined in (equation e:Gell of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 10 of arXiv:2608.30668, 'Recurrence and capacity of stable branching random walks'] Assume that \(d> \frac{2\alpha}{\alpha-1}\) and \(\theta\) has a finite
moment of order \(d-1\), then we have

\begin{equation*}{
G(x) \sim_{x \to \infty} C_{\mu,\theta} \lVert x \rVert^{d} g_{\theta}(x)^{\alpha}L(g_{\theta}(x)^{-1}),
}\end{equation*}
where \(C_{\mu,\theta} = \bcp(\{ 0 \})^{\alpha-1}\times  \int_{\R^d} \frac{1}{\Vert z\Vert^{d-2}} \frac{1}{\Vert u-z\Vert^{(\alpha-1)(d-2)}}\dd z\)
with \(u\) any unitary vector (for \(\lVert \cdot \rVert\)). In particular, \(G\) is \((2\alpha-d(\alpha-1))\)-varying. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop.CLT_M of arXiv:2606.13442, 'Scaling limit of additive functionals for reversible non-gradient exclusion process: criti'] For $d = 2$, let $\lambda = \frac{1}{N}$ and $m$ satisfy 
		\begin{equation}
			1 \ll 3^{18m} \ll \log N,
		\end{equation}
		(e.g. $m=\lfloor\tfrac{1}{36} \log \log N\rfloor$), then the martingale $\widetilde{M}^{N}_t$ defined in (equation eq.defMt_newTilde of the paper) converges weakly to a Brownian motion of variance $\sigma^2=\tfrac{\chi(\rho)}{2\pi\sqrt{\det[\mathbf{D}(\rho)]}}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary cor:week_converence of arXiv:2603.27906, 'Marked GUE-corners process in doubly periodic dimer models'] Let~$(u_{s,j}^t)_{1\leq s\leq t\leq 2\ell N}$ be the interlacing particles system defined in Definition~(def:marked_interlacing of the paper), and let~$\tau $ and~$\sigma^2$ be defined in~(equation eq:turning_point of the paper) and~(equation eq:sigma_square of the paper). Then
\begin{equation}
\left(\frac{u_{s,j}^{t}-2N\tau }{2\sigma\sqrt{N}}\right)_{1\leq s\leq t}\to \left(\xi_{s,j}^t\right)_{1\leq s\leq t},
\end{equation}
in the sense of weak convergence as~$N\to \infty$, and~$(\xi_{s,j}^t)_{1\leq s\leq t}$ is the marked GUE-corners process with~$\theta$ given in~(equation eq:theta of the paper). That is, for any compactly supported continuous functions~$\varphi:\Lambda_{\{0,1\}}\to [0,1]$,
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to \infty}\mathbb{E}_{\operatorname{Int}}\left[\prod_v \left(1-\varphi\left(t_v,\frac{u_{s_v,j_v}^{t_v}-2 N\tau }{2\sigma \sqrt{N}},j_v\right)\right)\right]
=\mathbb{E}_{\operatorname{GUE}}^\theta\left[\prod_v (1-\varphi(t_v,\xi_{s_v,j_v}^{t_v},j_v))\right],
\end{equation}
where~$\mathbb{E}_{\operatorname{Int}}$ and~$\mathbb{E}_{\operatorname{GUE}}^\theta$ are expectations of~$\mathbb{P}_{\operatorname{Int}}$ and~$\mathbb{P}_{\operatorname{GUE}}^\theta$, respectively.

