[Corollary 3 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] Let~$\{T_k\}_{k\ge0}$ have the law of $\{\frac12|B_k|^2\}_{k\ge0}$ for~$B$ denoting a standard two-dimen\-sional Brownian motion. Let~$\{Z_{T_k}\}_{k\ge0}$  be a sequence of random Borel measures on~$[0,1]$ coupled with~$\{T_k\}_{k\ge0}$ so that \eqref{E:1.21a} holds as stated. Then
\begin{equation}

\ZZ(\textd x)\laweq \sum_{k\ge1}1_{ [b^{-k-1},b^{-k})}(x)\, b^{-2k} Z_{T_k}\bigl(b^k\textd x\bigr). 
\end{equation}
A.e.\ sample of~$\ZZ$ is diffuse. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem theo:intro of arXiv:2605.21265, 'The non-Hermitian minor process'] Consider the complex Ginibre minor process $X^{(xN)}$, and fix any $x,y\in (0,1]$ with $x\le y$. Let $f,g$ be smooth functions supported in the open disks\footnote{Here $\mathbf{D}_x:=\{z\in\mathbf{C}:|z|\le \sqrt{x}\}$.} $\mathbf{D}_x$, $\mathbf{D}_y$, respectively. Then, the linear statistics $L^{(xN)}(f), L^{(yN)}(g)$ converge jointly to centered Gaussian random variables $L(x,f), L(y,g)$, with covariance
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{E} L(x,f) L(y,g)= -\frac{1}{\pi^2} \int_{\mathbf{D}_x} \!\!\mathrm{d}^2 z\int_{\mathbf{D}_y}\!\!\mathrm{d}^2 w\,\, \overline{\partial_z f(z)}\partial_w g(w) \partial_z\partial_{\overline{w}} \log K\big((x,z),(y,w)\big),

\end{equation}
where $K$ is given by\footnote{Later in (equation eq:def_K of the paper) we extend the definition of $K$ to general $x,y\in(0,1]$.}
\begin{equation}
K\big((x,z),(y,w)\big):=|z-w|^2+|x-y|\left(1-\frac{|w|^2}{y}\right), \qquad z\in \mathbf{D}_x, \; w\in \mathbf{D}_y.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Cons 8 of arXiv:2606.08115, '2606.08115'] There exists a constant $C$ such that for every $\ov{x} \in K$ 
\begin{equation}

V(M\ov{x}) \le C u(M\ov{x} + \ov{x}_0).
\end{equation}

