[Lemma 155 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] There exists a unique $K$-dimensional vector $\boldsymbol {Q}^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}\in\L'$, such that
 \begin{itemize}
  \item the segments $[\hat a_k'(\boldsymbol{Q}^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}),\hat b_k' (\boldsymbol {Q}^{\boldsymbol{\mu}})]$ indexed by $k \in [K]$ are pairwise disjoint;
  \item $\forall k \in [K] \quad [\alpha_k,\beta_k]\subseteq (\hat a_k'(\boldsymbol{Q}^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}),\hat b_k' (\boldsymbol{Q}^{\boldsymbol{\mu}})) \subseteq (\hat a'_{h^k}, \hat b'_{h^k})$;
 \item $\forall k \in [K] \quad \hat n_k(\boldsymbol{Q}^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}) = \mu([\hat a'_k(\boldsymbol{Q}^{\boldsymbol{\mu}}),\hat b'_k (\boldsymbol{Q}^{\boldsymbol{\mu}})])$.
 \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

