[Theorem thm:ergod of arXiv:2606.00325, 'Ergodicity for a class of stochastic Hasegawa-Mima equations with dissipation'] \textbf{(Uniqueness of the invariant measure)}
    Assume (ass of the paper), (ass_noise of the paper). There exists $N>0$ such that if $(I-\Delta)P^N(V)\subset \operatorname{span}\left(\{f_1, \ldots f_M\}\right)$, then there exists a unique ergodic invariant measure $\mu$ for the equation (equation eq:main of the paper) on $V$, which is supported on $W$. Moreover if $\phi_t$ is a stationary process with respect to this invariant measure, it holds 
    \begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}\left[ \left|A^{1/2} \phi(t)\right|^2\right] = \frac{\kappa}{2} \qquad \forall t\ge 0.
    \end{equation}
    If (ass2 of the paper) holds, the unique invariant measure is supported on $(I-\Delta)^{-1}D(B^{1/2})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:exp-euler-gaussian of arXiv:2608.25279, 'Provable Non-Acceleration of Standard Strang\\ Splittings of Kinetic Langevin Dynamics'] Let $\kappa>1$ and let $h,\gamma>0$. For
$U_\lambda(x)=\lambda x^2/2$ on $\mathbb R$, let $M_\lambda$ be the
matrix in (equation eq:exp-euler-matrix of the paper). If $M_\kappa$ is not Schur
stable, then the left-endpoint exponential integrator for
$U_\kappa$ has no invariant probability law. If $M_\kappa$ is
Schur stable, then $M_\lambda$ is Schur stable for every
$\lambda\in[1,\kappa]$, and
\begin{equation}
 \rho(M_1)\ge1-\frac{64}{\kappa}.
\end{equation}
In this case, the chain for $U_1$ has a unique invariant law
$\pi_1$. Let $P$ denote the transition kernel of the chain for
$U_1$. For every initial state $y\in\mathbb R^2$,
\begin{equation}
 \limsup_{n\to\infty}
 \lVert \delta_yP^n-\pi_1\rVert_{\mathrm{TV}}^{1/n}
 \le\rho(M_1),
\end{equation}
and the limit exists and equals $\rho(M_1)$ for a suitable $y$.
Moreover, there are initial states $y,\widetilde y\in\mathbb R^2$ such
that
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{n\to\infty}
 \norm{\delta_yP^n-\delta_{\widetilde y}P^n}_{\mathrm{TV}}^{1/n}
 =\rho(M_1)\ge1-\frac{64}{\kappa}.
\end{equation}

