[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2606.21111, 'On Elephant Random Walk with Delayed Amnesia'] Let $\{M_n, \mathcal{F}_n\}_{n\ge1}$ be a locally square-integrable martingale in
$\mathbb{R}^{d}$ with predictable
quadratic variation $\{\langle M \rangle_n\}_{n\ge1}$.
Also, let $\{V_n\}_{n\ge1}$ be a sequence of non-random square matrices of order $d$ such that $\|V_n\|$ decreases to $0$ as $n\to \infty$. Assume that there exists a symmetric and positive semi-definite matrix $V$ such that
\begin{equation}
V_n \langle M \rangle_n V_n^t
\xrightarrow{p} V.
\end{equation}
Moreover, assume that Lindeberg's condition is satisfied, that is, for all $\varepsilon>0$,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{k=1}^{n}
\mathbb{E}(
\|V_n \Delta M_k\|^{2}
\mathbb{I}_{\{\|V_n\Delta M_k\|>\varepsilon\}}| \mathcal{F}_{k-1})
\xrightarrow{p} 0,
\end{equation}
where $\Delta M_n=M_n-M_{n-1}$, $M_0=0$. Then, $V_n M_n
\xrightarrow{d}
\mathcal{N}(0,V)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition prop:equiv_weak of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $v:(0,T)\to L^{1}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, and let also $\sigma$, $b$ be as in (equation eq:sig_b_deg_den of the paper) and $\nu \in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. If either
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] Assumption (assum:b_0 of the paper)-(b) is satisfied,
\item[(ii)] or, Assumption (assum:b_0 of the paper)-(b-loc) is satisfied and $v\in L^{\infty}_{T}L^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^d)$,
 \end{itemize}
then  $v$ is a \emph{pseudo-mild solution} to (equation eq:nl_fp_cp_den_kin of the paper) if and only if it is a weak solution to D-NL-FP$(b,\sigma; \nu)$ (see Definition (def:sol_nl_fp_den of the paper)). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:weighted_entropy_tail of arXiv:2605.15401, 'Noise Tradeoffs, Stationary Information Flow, and Structural Balance in Unit-Birth Network'] Let $\pi$ be a probability distribution on $S=\mathbb{Z}_{\ge0}^N$ with full support satisfying
$\mathbb{E}_\pi[s(X)^2]<\infty$, where $s(x)=\|x\|_1$. Then
\begin{equation}
\sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)\,(1+s(x))\,|\log\pi(x)|<\infty.
\end{equation}
Moreover, if $\Lambda:S\to[0,\infty)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}

\Lambda(x)\le C_\Lambda(1+s(x))\qquad \forall x\in S,
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
\sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)\,\Lambda(x)\,\bigl(1+|\log\pi(x)|\bigr)<\infty.
\end{equation}

[Proposition 24 (Compact reduction and path-law pushforward) of arXiv:2609.02196, 'Schr\"odinger Bridges on Lie Group Manifolds for Probabilistic Intrinsic Generation'] Under the reduced-representation assumption in Appendix~\ref{app:reduction-details}, the stochastic learning problem is an RCCBM problem on \(\G_{\rm red}\). If the reconstruction is a \(C^2\)-diffeomorphism onto the modeled conformation manifold, the induced path map preserves relative entropy,
\begin{equation}
\KL(P_{\rm red}^{\rm SB}\|\widehat P_{\rm red})
=\KL((\mathbf\Phi_{\rm red})_\#P_{\rm red}^{\rm SB}
\|(\mathbf\Phi_{\rm red})_\#\widehat P_{\rm red}).
\end{equation}
Without injectivity, only the data-processing inequality
\begin{equation}
\KL((\mathbf{\mathcal R}_{\rm red})_\#P\|
(\mathbf{\mathcal R}_{\rm red})_\#Q)\leq\KL(P\|Q)
\end{equation}
is available. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

