Let n and r be positive integers. Among all finite simple n-vertex graphs containing no K_{r+1}, the maximum number of edges is e(T_r(n)), attained uniquely up to isomorphism by the Turan graph T_r(n), the complete r-partite graph whose part sizes differ by at most one. Consequently e(G) <= e(T_r(n)) <= (1-1/r)n^2/2; the second inequality is an equality when r divides n. For r = 2 this yields Mantel's bound e(G) <= floor(n^2/4).
