[Theorem thm:KS of arXiv:2510.13572, 'Coalescence in Markov chains'] Let $P=(p_{i,j}:i,j\in S)$.
Let $\mathcal S$ be a partition of $S$, and let
\begin{equation}
\lambda_{r,s}^{(i)}= \sum_{j \in S_s} p_{i,j}, \quad i \in S_r.
\end{equation}
A Markov chain $X$ with transition matrix $P$ 

 is $\mathcal S$-lumpable if and only if, 
\begin{equation}
\text{for every $r$, $s$, we have that $\lambda_{r,s} := \lambda_{r,s}^{(i)}$ is constant 
for $i\in S_r$.}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop 11 (Exponential tightness) of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] There exists a sequence of compact sets $K_L \subset \mathcal{M}_1(\mathbb{R})$ ($L > 0$) such that

\begin{align}

\limsup_{L \to +\infty} \limsup_{N \to +\infty} \frac{1}{N} \ln \overline{\Pi}_{N}[K_L^c] = - \infty \, .

\end{align}

Likewise, if we let $K_{L,\mathsf{c}} = K_L \cap \mathcal{M}_{1,\mathsf{c}}(\mathbb{R})$
then (we claim, according to our earlier choice of $K_L$) $K_{L,\mathsf{c}}$
is compact in the subspace topology of $\mathcal{M}_{1,\mathsf{c}}(\mathbb{R})$

\begin{align}

\limsup_{L \to +\infty} \limsup_{N \to +\infty} \frac{1}{N} \ln \overline{\Pi}_{N,\mathsf{c}}[K_{L,\mathsf{c}}^c] = - \infty \, .

\end{align}

where here the complement is taken in $\mathcal{M}_{1,\mathsf{c}}(\mathbb{R})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2606.14812, 'Joint convergence in Wiener chaos via transport hierarchy and Malliavin covariances'] Let $ (f_{N}, N\geq 1)$ be a sequence of random variables in $ H ^{\odot p}$ with $p\geq 2$. Assume (\ref{a1}). Then
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item  For every $h\in H$,
			\begin{equation*}
				f_{N} \otimes _{1} h \to _{N \to \infty} 0 \mbox{ in } H ^{\otimes p-1}.
			\end{equation*}
			\item For any sequence $(h_{N}, N\geq 1)$ bounded in $H$, 
			\begin{equation*}
				f_{N}\otimes _{1} h_{N} \to _{N \to \infty} 0 \mbox{ in } H ^{\otimes p-1}.
			\end{equation*}
		\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma Delta theta denominator stays positive of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] Let $(\lambda,C^2)\in S^0$ with $\Delta z(\lambda,C^2)=0$. Then 
  \begin{equation*}
    k(s)^{2p-2}((p-1-\lambda)^2 + p^2 C^2)-p^2C^2\geq \varepsilon_{\lambda,C^2}>0.
  \end{equation*}

