[Theorem 10 (Critical logarithmic estimate) of arXiv:2608.17540, 'Pathwise stability for one-dimensional SDEs driven by Brownian motion and a symmetric stab'] Assume Assumptions~\ref{ass:baseline}--\ref{ass:solutions} and $B,A,S<1$. Suppose $\wt\eta=1/\alpha$ and set
\[
  \Theta:=\max\{B,A,S\}.
\]
Then there exists $C>0$ such that, for $0<\Theta<1$,
\begin{equation}
\sup_{0\le t\le T}\E|X_t-\wt X_t|^{\alpha-1}
\le C\left[|x_0-\wt x_0|^{\alpha-1}+\left(\log\frac1\Theta\right)^{-1}\right].
\end{equation}
When $\Theta=0$, the right-hand side is understood as $C|x_0-\wt x_0|^{\alpha-1}$ by the limiting convention.

[Lemma lm:B-cK of arXiv:2507.08757, 'Strong existence and uniqueness of the tilt-indexed Busemann process in the planar corner '] Fix ${\widehat B}\in\widehat{\mathcal{K}}$. 
Suppose $s\in(0,1]$ satisfies 
	\begin{align}
	\mathbb{P}\bigl( \forall u,v\in\mathbb{Z}^2:{\boldsymbol{\pi}}^{{\widehat B},u,s}\curlywedgeuparrow {\boldsymbol{\pi}}^{{\widehat B},v,s}\hspace{0.7pt}\bigr) =1.
	\end{align}
Then $A^s$, defined by (equation B-def of the paper), is a shift-covariant recovering cocycle. For any $u\in\mathbb{Z}^2$ and $\{x,y\}\subset u+\mathbb{Z}^2_+$, $A^s(x,y)$ is $\mathcal{F}^{+}_u$-measurable, up to events of zero $\mathbb{P}$-measure.

In season k, the fraction of infected hosts in immunity class j, z_j^{(k)}, and total attack ratio z^{(k)} = sum_{j=1}^r p_j^{(k)} z_j^{(k)} satisfy 1 - z_j^{(k)} = exp(-(1 - (1 - delta_k) * iota_j^{(k)}) * tau_k * z^{(k)}). The scalar attack ratio satisfies 1 - z = sum_{j=1}^r p_j^{(k)} exp(-(1 - (1 - delta_k) iota_j^{(k)}) tau_k z). A strictly positive solution z^{(k)} in (0, 1) exists and is unique if and only if R_E^{(k)} > 1; if R_E^{(k)} <= 1, the unique solution in [0, 1] is z^{(k)} = 0.
Host distribution p^{(k)} in Delta^{r-1}
tau_k > 0 and delta_k in [0, 1]

[Defn 96 of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Let $k \in \mathbb N$ and $1\leq i\neq j \leq k$. Consider a decreasing function $F:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ of exponential decay at infinity. We define the functional $V^{ij}(F;\bullet):(I^k)^{\mathbb Z_{\ge 0}} \to I^{\mathbb Z_{\ge 0}}$ by 
  \begin{align}

      V^{ij}(F;r):= \sum_{s=0}^{r-1} F(|   R^i_{s}-   R^j_{s}|).
  \end{align}
We remark that the processes $V^{ij}(F;\bullet)$ are predictable with respect to the canonical filtration on $(I^k)^{\mathbb Z_{\ge 0}}$.

[Lemma l:conv of arXiv:2604.03979, 'Stationary Distributions in Monotone Markov Models: Theory and Applications'] There exists an $x^* \in \mathsf{X}$ such that

    \begin{equation}
         d(T_t \, \bar x, x^*) \to 0
         \quad \text{as} \quad
         t \to \infty.
    \end{equation}

[Thm Main Theorem Statement of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] Fix a $k \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $\{a_j\}_{j=1}^n, \{b_j\}_{j=1}^{n-1}$ be the tridiagonal entry (processes) as in (equation eq:tridiagonalmatrix of the paper), from the $n\times n$ tridiagonalized GOE process. Define 
\begin{equation*}
E_n(t):=\left(a_1(t),\dots, a_k(t), \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}(b_1(t)^2-n),\dots ,\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}(b_k(t)^2-n)\right).
\end{equation*} 
Then, $E_n(t)$ converges weakly in $C[0,\infty)$ to a vector process $E(t)=(A_1(t),\dots,A_k(t),B_1(t),\dots,B_k(t))$ as $n\to\infty$, where
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}E(t) \sim \mathbf{OU}_{2k}(1,3,\dots,2k-1,2,4,\dots,2k).
\end{equation*} 
More explicitly, the components $A_j(t) \in C[0,\infty)$ and $B_j(t) \in C[0,\infty)$ are jointly-independent centered Ornstein--Uhlenbeck processes with covariances given as $\mathrm{Cov}(A_j(t),A_j(s))=2e^{-(2j-1)|t-s|}$ and $\mathrm{Cov}(B_j(t),B_j(s))=2e^{-2j|t-s|}$, for $j=1,\dots,k$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2607.13273, 'Asymptotics of transition densities for L\'evy processes with stochastic resetting'] For every $\lambda < m$ we have 
