{"artifact_digest":"sha256:db8367b3084f6b729af99cdfed34503b1617b347a49c8c9595258bb38360cfa2","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/db/db8367b3084f6b729af99cdfed34503b1617b347a49c8c9595258bb38360cfa2.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:9868f09f3c09fc7c492d1d5a21653694ed1a1961a29d22a38a64e7b6228ad594","bundle_entry_key":"2607-25616-theorem-16-spectral-decay-lattice-inducti","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_112","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_112.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-25616-corollary-16-spectral-decay-lattice-inducti","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Choose $n_0$ so that the assumed estimate holds for
$\varepsilon\leq2^{-n_0}$. Decomposing into dyadic annuli gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
    \int_{0<|\xi|<2^{-n_0}}
    \frac{1}{\xi^2}\,d\sigma_\eta(\xi)
    &\leq
    \sum_{n\geq n_0}
    2^{2n+2}
    \sigma_\eta([-2^{-n},2^{-n}])\\
    &\leq
    4C\sum_{n\geq n_0}2^{-\alpha n}
    <\infty.
\end{aligned}
\]
