[Lemma lem:1530 of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] Consider the triples $(x_i,y_i,D_i)$ with $x_i\in D_i$ and $y_i\in \partial D_i$ that belong to one of the following three cases
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
        \item $x_i\in\partial\mathcal{B}_{-1}$ and $D_i=\mathcal B_l^\circ\setminus \mathcal B_{-4}$ with $l=2,3,4$.
        \item $x_i\in\partial\mathcal{B}_{-1}$ and $D_i=\mathcal B_2^\circ$.
        \item $x_i\in\partial\mathcal{B}_{2}$ and $D_i=\mathcal B_l^\circ\setminus \mathcal B_{-1}$ with $l=5,6,7$.
    \end{enumerate}
   Let $\eta_i$ be sampled according to $\mathbb{P}_{x_i\to y_i}^{D_i}$ and $\overline\eta:=\cup_{i=1}^k \eta_i$. Then, for each $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $0<C_1=C_1(\varepsilon,k)<\infty$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        \prod_{i=1}^k\mathbb P_{x_i\to y_i}^{D_i}\Big(K(p_{1/2,1}^{\overline\eta\cup\mathcal B_0})<C_1\Big)>1-\varepsilon.
    \end{equation}

