[Thm dBM of arXiv:2411.14090, '{\bf Exponential Ergodicity in $\W_1$ for SDEs with Distribution Dependent Noise and Parti'] Suppose that {\bf(A2)} holds with
\begin{equation}
(K_1+L_b)\gamma^{-2}\leq \frac{K_1}{2(K_1+L_b)}.

\end{equation}
Then there exists a constant $\delta_0>0$ such that when $\kappa<\delta_0$, (equation Langevin of the paper) has a unique invariant probability measure $\mu^\ast\in\mathbb P_1(\mathbb R^{2d})$ and there exist constants $c,\lambda>0$ such that
\begin{equation*}
  \mathbb W_1(P_t^*\eta, \mu^\ast)\leq c
   \text{\rm{e}}^{-\lambda t}
  \mathbb W_1(\eta, \mu^\ast),\quad \eta\in\mathscr P_1(\mathbb R^{2d}),\, t\geq 0.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 39 (Projection and gate decomposition) of arXiv:2607.26377, '\textbf{Entire Logarithmic Signatures\\ of Bounded-Variation Paths in Finite Dimensions}'] Let $(\mathscr{T},d)$ be a complete metric tree and let $C\subset\mathscr{T}$ be nonempty, closed, and convex. Every $x\in\mathscr{T}$ has a unique metric projection $p\in C$. Moreover, $p\in[x,c]$ for every $c\in C$. If $p$ and $q$ are the projections of $x$ and $y$, respectively, and $p\neq q$, then
\begin{equation}
[x,y]=[x,p]\cup[p,q]\cup[q,y],
\end{equation}
where consecutive pieces meet only at their common endpoint and $[x,p]\cap[q,y]=\varnothing$.

