[Theorem 39 (Discrete Gaussian for extended filling fractions) of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Suppose that Assumptions~\ref{Assumptions_Theta}, \ref{Assumptions_basic}, \ref{Assumptions_offcrit}, \ref{Assumptions_analyticity} hold and that the equilibrium measure has at least one saturation. There exists $\eps > 0$ and random $K$-tuples of integers $\boldsymbol{Q}^{\circ},\boldsymbol{N}^{\circ}$ and $K$-tuple of real numbers $\hat{\boldsymbol{a}}^{\circ},\hat{\boldsymbol{b}}^{\circ}$ such that the following properties hold with probability exponentially close to $1$ as $\N \rightarrow \infty$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\boldsymbol{N}^{\circ},\hat{\boldsymbol{a}}^{\circ}$, $\hat{\boldsymbol{b}}^{\circ}$ are deterministic affine functions of $\boldsymbol{Q}^{\circ}$, with coefficients of the linear part depending only on the diagonal of $\boldsymbol{\Theta}$;
\item the segment $[\hat{a}_k^{\circ},\hat{b}_k^{\circ}]$ contains the $k$-th band and is contained in its $\eps$-neighborhood;
\item the segment $[\N\hat{a}_k^{\circ},\N\hat{b}_k^{\circ}]$ contains exactly $N_k^{\circ}$ particles, the leftmost and rightmost ones being at integer distance of its endpoints.
\end{enumerate}
Besides, the distribution of $\boldsymbol{Q}^{\circ}$ is asymptotically equal to a discrete Gaussian distribution $\textnormal{\textsf{\textbf{Gau\ss{}}}}_{\amsmathbb{Z}}$ as $\N \rightarrow \infty$, whose quadratic form $\boldsymbol{B}$ and centering vector $\boldsymbol{u}$ depend on the parameters of the ensemble. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Thm -1 of arXiv:2411.13403, 'Path weighting method for spot sensitivities and its variance reduction'] If $F(S)$ has generalised derivatives with at most
polynomial growth a.e., then the random variables 
\begin{equation}
\xi=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}}\Big(F(S)\Theta^{(1)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}Z_{1})-F(\hat{S})\Theta^{(1)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}\hat{Z}_{1})\Big),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\eta_{1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}}\Big(F(S)\Lambda^{(1)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}Z_{1})-F(\hat{S})\Lambda^{(1)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}\hat{Z}_{1})\Big),
\end{equation}
have variance of order
\begin{align}
\mathrm{Var}(\xi) & =\mathrm{O}(1),\;(\Delta t_{1}\to0),\\
\mathrm{Var}(\eta_{1}) & =\mathrm{O}(1),\;(\Delta t_{1}\to0).
\end{align}
If, in addition, $F(S)$ has second order generalised derivatives
with at most polynomial growth a.e., then the random variable
\begin{equation}
\eta_{2}=\frac{1}{\Delta t_{1}}\Big(F(S)\Lambda^{(2)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}Z_{1})-2F(\bar{S})\Lambda^{(2)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}\bar{Z}_{1})+F(\hat{S})\Lambda^{(2)}(s,Z_{1},\sqrt{\Delta t_{1}}\hat{Z}_{1})\Big)
\end{equation}
has variance of order
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Var}(\eta_{1})=\mathrm{O}(1),\;(\Delta t_{1}\to0),
\end{equation}

Let f be an orientation-preserving homeomorphism of the circle and let F be any lift of f to the real line. The limit rho(F) = lim_n (F^n(x) - x)/n exists, is independent of x, and its class modulo 1 - the rotation number rho(f) - is independent of the lift; it is invariant under topological conjugacy by an orientation-preserving homeomorphism. Moreover rho(f) is RATIONAL if and only if f has a periodic orbit, and when rho(f) = p/q in lowest terms every periodic orbit has period exactly q. When rho(f) is irrational, f has no periodic point, every orbit has the same combinatorial order as an orbit of R_{rho}, and f is semi-conjugate to the rigid rotation R_{rho}; the semi-conjugacy is a conjugacy exactly when f has no wandering interval.
Orientation-preserving and merely a HOMEOMORPHISM - no smoothness is used here;
smoothness enters only in Denjoy's theorem to upgrade the semi-conjugacy to a conjugacy.

[Lemma L_strTWB of arXiv:2510.26724, 'Sharp Fuss-Catalan thresholds in graph bootstrap percolation'] Let $T$ be a TWG and $S\subsetneq T$ a partial TWG. Then, 
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathcal E(S)\geqslant 0$, with equality 
if and only $S$ is obtained from $T$ 
by removing one of its branches; 
\item $\mathcal D(S,T)\leqslant c\mathcal E(S)+2$; and 
\item $\mathcal T(S)\leqslant c\mathcal E(S)$, 
\end{enumerate}
for some constant $c$ depending only on $r$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

