    Assume that $f_n \to f$ pointwise at continuity points of $f$.
    Fix $\varepsilon>0$.
    Since $f$ is nondecreasing, almost every point is a continuity point and we can construct a sequence $0=x_0 < x_1 < \dots < x_{\ell-1} < x_\ell=1$ of continuity points (except maybe $x_\ell$, but this does not matter in the following) which is a subdivision of $[0,1]$ with steps bounded by $\varepsilon$.
    For each $k<\ell$, $G_f \cap \left( (x_k,x_{k+1}]\times[0,1] \right)$ is a continuous nondecreasing curve from $(x_k, f(x_k))$ to $(x_{k+1}, f(x_{k+1}))$, and thus its $\varepsilon$-halo covers the rectangle $[x_k, x_{k+1}] \times [f(x_k)-\varepsilon, ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:f1f07be4859a77c40931acb6d683abdeb0f843b5a8f94943f80f582411bd771c","revision_digest":"sha256:d3c96b3cd45a2f95138e4ccc17c2a986a0effcb8fd5068ef88a3b38637a51227","source_id":"arxiv:2604.23751","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

