{"artifact_digest":"sha256:b57d78f414360ffefebc2cddb8c43e768f83d372c1818003b9ba9227128fd4ec","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/b5/b57d78f414360ffefebc2cddb8c43e768f83d372c1818003b9ba9227128fd4ec.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:962162b6aeae1816bd6785e3917c69d2cf5eac1a2b886d6ebaa0e26369165400","bundle_entry_key":"2412-01995-lemma-12-e2","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_116","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_116.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2412-01995-lemma-12-e2","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: In Lemma~(lem:e1 of the paper), we already have  $\lVert g^n \rVert_{C^1(\bar \Omega_N)} \leq K(N)$. We prove that for any $\xi \in \mathcal{S}^{d-1}$, $$\textstyle \sup_{x \in \bar \Omega_N}|g^n_{\xi \xi}(x)| \leq K(N).$$ 

In the rest of proof, let us omit the superscript $n$ from $g^n$. Take $C=N+10$, we consider the maximization problem 
\begin{align*}
