[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2606.08325, 'Digit Mixing under Polynomial Maps'] Let \(P\in\mathbb R[x]\) have degree \(d\ge2\).
			There is a constant $\Gamma_0=\Gamma_0(d)$ only depending on the degree of $P$ such that the following holds. Suppose that for all sufficiently large $n$,
			\begin{equation}
				v_n\ge (\log n)^{\Gamma_0} n^{-(d-1)/d}.
			\end{equation}
			Then $P(X)$ is  almost surely absolutely normal. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2606.11271, '\LARGE The $K$-th nearest neighbor random walk on a Poisson point process gets trapped'] On $\cE_n$, there exist $m \in \{1,\dots,n-1\}$, $T \in \cT_m$ and an embedding $x$ of $T$ such that the following holds.
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item For all vertices $v$ of $T$, $|B(x(v),r_M(x(v)) \cap \chi| = M+1$.
		\item For all edges $v \to w$ of $T$, $\|x(w)-x(v)\| \le r_M(x(v))$.
		\item $An \le M \sum_{v} r_M(x(v))$.
	\end{enumerate}

