[Defn def:root-biased of arXiv:2601.00119, 'The largest common subtree of two random trees'] Let $\mu$ be a critical measure on $\mathbb{N}$. A random plane tree $\tau_*$ is called a \emph{root-biased Bienaym\'{e} tree with offspring distribution $\mu$} if it satisfies the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(a)] For all $k \geq 0$, $\mathbf{P}(\mathrm{k}_\varnothing(\tau_*) = k) = (k+1)\mu(k+1)$.
    \item[(b)] For all $k \geq 1$ such that $\mathbf{P}(\mathrm{k}_\varnothing(\tau_*) = k) > 0$, conditionally given that $\mathrm{k}_{\varnothing}(\tau_*) = k$, $\theta_1\tau,...,\theta_k\tau$ are independent Bienaym\'{e} trees with offspring distribution $\mu$.
\end{enumerate}

[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2608.09419, 'Left-tail expansions for Schr\"oder branching processes with explicit convergence rates'] \lb{L2}
	Let $\mP_{p,E}$ be the set of polynomial probability generating functions with the expectation $P'(1)=E$ and, also, satisfying $P(0)=0$, $P'(0)=p$. Denote $N=\lfloor \frac{E-p}{1-p}\rfloor$. Then, the unique minimizer of the second derivative at $z=1$ is
	\[\lb{110}
	{\rm argmin}_{P\in\mP_{p,E}}P''(1)=pz+(N(1-p)+1-E)z^N+(E-p-N(1-p))z^{N+1}.
	\]
It is assumed that $E\ge 2-p$, because, otherwise, $\mP_{p,E}=\varnothing$, and \er{110} does not make sense. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:geometric of arXiv:2411.10419, 'Lower bounds on the top Lyapunov exponent for linear PDEs driven by the 2D stochastic Navi'] For any $ \ell \in \mathbf{R}^{2} $ define $ Q_{\ell} (k) = || \ell |^{2} - 2 \langle k, \ell
\rangle |^{2} | \langle k^{\perp}, \ell \rangle |^{2}$.
Then there exists a $ c > 0 $ such that for all $ k \in \mathbf{Z}^{2} $
\begin{equ}
\sum_{| \ell | \leqslant \sqrt{2}} Q_{\ell}(k) \geqslant c | k |^{4} \;.
\end{equ}

[Corollary 25 of arXiv:2608.12291, 'When should one stop the most exciting game?\\ {\tiny Sequential Inference for win-marting'] Let $T<\infty$. The function $(t,l)\mapsto \tilde W_T(t,l)$ is $\llip{\tilde{\mathcal{S}}_T}$. Specifically, at points of differentiability it holds that
    \begin{equation}
        \partial_l \tilde{W}_T(t,l)=\mathbb{E}\left[\int_{0}^{\tilde\tau^*}\partial_l L^l_{u}\partial_l(\tilde{\mathcal{L}}\tilde{g})(L^l_{u})\,du\right]
    \end{equation}
    where $\tilde\tau^*:=\tilde\tau^*_T(t,l)$ is the optimal stopping time, and 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}[c(t+\tilde\tau^*)]-c(t)\leq \partial_t\tilde{W}_T(t,l)\leq  \mathbb{E}[c(t+\tilde\tau^*)]-c(t) + K''\mathbb{P}(\tilde\tau^*=T-t),
    \end{equation}
     where $K''$ is defined in Proposition \ref{prop:time_lip}.

[Lemma 56 of arXiv:2609.03070, 'Variants of order semicontinuity in Banach lattices'] Let $E\subset F$ be a super order dense sublattice and let $B\subset F$ be solid. The following sets are equal:
\item[(i)] The set of all $f\in F$ such that there is $Q\subset B_{+}$ with $Q\uparrow\left|f\right|$;
\item[(i')] The set of all $f\in F$ such that there is $Q\subset B_{+}\cap E$ with $Q\uparrow\left|f\right|$;
\item[(ii)] The order adherence of $B$ in $F$;
\item[(ii')] The order adherence of $B\cap E$ in $F$;
\item[(iii)] The uo adherence of $B$ in $F$;
\item[(iii')] The uo adherence of $B\cap E$ in $F$.\medskip

This set is solid and its intersection with $E$ is the common $\sigma$-order and $\mathrm{u\sigma o}$ adherence of $B\cap E$ in $E$. The $\sigma$-order and $\mathrm{u\sigma o}$ closures of $B$ and $B\cap E$ also coincide, and are a solid set. Both the adherence and the closure are convex, whenever $B$ is. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition ito-formula of arXiv:2606.00325, 'Ergodicity for a class of stochastic Hasegawa-Mima equations with dissipation'] \textbf{(It\={o}-Formula for $\|\phi\|^2_{V}$})
Let $\phi(t)$ be a solution of (equation eq:main of the paper) in the sense of (def-sol-H1 of the paper), then it holds 
\begin{align*}
   |\phi(t)|^2 + |\nabla \phi(t)|^2 + 2\int_0^t|A^{1/2} \phi(s)|^2ds = |\phi_0|^2 + |\nabla \phi_0|^2 + \kappa t + 2\int_0^t\left\langle\phi(s), dW_s\right\rangle,
\end{align*}
where $\kappa := \sum_k \sigma_k^2|(I-\Delta)^{-1/2}f_k|^2$.

