[Proposition eq:Breg of arXiv:2412.06420, 'Accuracy, Estimates, and Representation Results'] If $\mathsf{acc}$ is proper, then $\varphi_\mathsf{acc}$ is convex and if it is differentiable, then:
		\begin{align}
		&\mathrm{Exp}_p\mathsf{acc}(p)-\mathrm{Exp}_p\mathsf{acc}(x)=\varphi_\mathsf{acc}(p)-\varphi_\mathsf{acc}(x)-(p-x)\varphi_\mathsf{acc}'(x)
		\end{align}
			If it is not differentiable, then we have the same form, but with $\varphi_\mathsf{acc}'$ as some sub-gradient. 

		Also for $v\in\set{0,1}$,
		\begin{align}
		&\mathsf{acc}_v(x)=\mathsf{acc}_v(v)-\br*{\varphi_\mathsf{acc}(v)-\varphi_\mathsf{acc}(x)-(v-x)\varphi_\mathsf{acc}'(x)}
		\end{align}
		And in fact
		\begin{align}
		&\mathsf{acc}_v(x)=\varphi_\mathsf{acc}(x)+(v-x)\varphi_\mathsf{acc}'(x)
		\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2606.31596, 'The Invariant Measure of Multiscale Markov Chains via Fast Arborescence Factorization'] The weight of the set $\mathcal T_{x_1}$ of arborescences of $K_{n+1}$ and the reduced weight of the set $\hat{\mathcal T}_{x_1}$ of arborescences of $\hat K_{n}$ are related by:
\begin{equation} 
	r\left(\mathcal{T}_{x_1}\right) \,=\, r^\textup{out}(s)\,\ \hat{r}\left(\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{x_1}\right) \,.
\end{equation}

