[Thm GenLineInt of arXiv:2506.18207, 'Cartan's Path Development, the Logarithmic Signature and a Conjecture of Lyons-Sidorova'] Under the assumptions of Theorem (thm:LineInt of the paper),
suppose further that $y_{1}=0$. Then one has
\begin{equation}
\int_{0}^{1}\Phi(d\gamma_{t})=0
\end{equation}
for all smooth one-forms $\Phi(x,y)=f(x,y)dx+g(x,y)dy$ on $\mathbb{R}^{2}$
which satisfy the following two conditions:

\vspace{2mm}\noindent (i)
$\Phi(x+1,y)=\Phi(x,y)$ for all $x,y\in\mathbb{R}$;

\vspace{2mm}\noindent (ii) $\int_{0}^{1}f(x,y)dx=0$ for all $y\in\mathbb{R}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

