{"artifact_digest":"sha256:430f9f0acf85279fb84b4a0c28c250445c8d480d0a1acf544cb0b2b1d3cc93ed","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/43/430f9f0acf85279fb84b4a0c28c250445c8d480d0a1acf544cb0b2b1d3cc93ed.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:b9c426bdf3a13197828dbcd603d890142cb2db556a567e196d9994f00245a6ed","bundle_entry_key":"2608-25971-lemma-3-taubiric-mean-convex","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_109","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_109.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-25971-lemma-3-taubiric-mean-convex","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Since the scalar curvature $\mathrm{Scal}=\sum_i\rho_i=2\sum_{i<j}K_{ij}$,
\begin{equation*}
 \sum_{i<j}P_{ij}^{(\tau)}
 =\left(n-1-\frac\tau2\right)\mathrm{Scal}.
\end{equation*}
The coefficient is positive for $n\geq3$ and $0\leq\tau\leq2$.  Thus
nonnegative $\tau$-bi-Ricci curvature implies $\mathrm{Scal}\geq0$, and
strict positivity implies $\mathrm{Scal}>0$.  The Gauss equation
\begin{equation*}
 \mathrm{Scal}=H^2-|A|^2-n(n-1)\kappa^2
\end{equation*}
and $|A|^2\geq H^2/n$ give $H^2\geq n^2\kappa^2$, with strict inequality in
the positive case.  Here $H$ is computed using any local unit normal, while
$H^2$ is the squared norm of the globally defined mean-curvature vector.  ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:d6f7024626cbe5694c512c215dcbbff0a637ae04db4d43892f15581cc2734ad6","revision_digest":"sha256:f5e3e4f4993a4f96a4af0ae3741bf4ffacf489d5730a31aae55bb23c9931e916","source_id":"arxiv:2608.25971","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

