[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2608.19179, 'A random walk on $p$-groups with a symmetric perfect pairing'] Suppose $1 \leq k \leq n$. If $M_{k,k}$ is non-singular, then there is a canonical isomorphism of groups equipped with a symmetric pairing:
\begin{equation}

V_k^{\perp} \Big/ V_k \cong \Ker(M_{k,k}) \times \Q_p^{n-k} 
\end{equation}
where the pairing on the right hand side is the sum of the pairing on $\Ker(M_{k,k})$, and some symmetric pairing on $\Q_p^{n-k}$. The pairing on the left hand side is the pairing induced by $M_{n,n}$.

[Prop mb:mvsde of arXiv:2411.11759, 'Milstein-type schemes for McKean--Vlasov SDEs driven by Brownian motion and Poisson random'] If Assumptions \mbox{\normalfont  (asum:ic of the paper)} to  \mbox{\normalfont (asum:con of the paper)}   are satisfied, then there exists a unique solution of  the McKean--Vlasov SDE (equation eq:sde of the paper) as well as of its associated interacting particle system (equation eq:int of the paper). 
		Further, when Assumption \mbox{\normalfont (asum:lin** of the paper)} also holds,  {then there exists a constant $K>0$ such that, for all $N\in \mathbb N$}
		\begin{align*}
		\sup_{t\in[0,T]}E|x_t|^{\bar p}\leq K \mbox{ and }\sup_{i\in\{1,\ldots,N\}}\sup_{t\in[0,T]}E|x_t^{i,N}|^{\bar p}\leq K.
		\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma shifting RW of arXiv:2411.14350, 'Randomized Geodesic Flow on Hyperbolic Groups'] Let $(X_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ be a random walk on a hyperbolic group $G$ with symmetric step distribution $\mu$ whose support is finite and generates $G$ and starting point $X_0=o$. Fix $m\in \mathbb{N}$ and set
\begin{align*}
\bar Z^m_n &:= X_m^{-1}X_{m-n} &&\text{ for } n=0,...,m;\\
Z^m_n &:= X_m^{-1}X_{m+n} &&\text{ for } n\geq m\\
\end{align*}
Then the following hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\big(\bar Z^m_n\big)_{n=0}^m$ and $\big(Z^m_n\big)_{i=0}^{\infty}$ are independent for any $m\in\mathbb{N}$. 
\item Furthermore, they converge in distribution to independent random walks $\big(\bar Z_n\big)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ and $\big(Z_n\big)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ with step distribution $\mu$ and starting point $o$.
\item Finally, $\big(\bar Z_n\big)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ and $\big(Z_n\big)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ converge a.s to points $Z_{\infty}$ and $Z_{-\infty}$ on $\partial G$.
\end{itemize}

