{"artifact_digest":"sha256:053cf7f8b673eae06001428752216c8a61737017e0fe06d2c3b41fa6ace32a44","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/05/053cf7f8b673eae06001428752216c8a61737017e0fe06d2c3b41fa6ace32a44.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:c60b4584ed6cc3b95885d29161924850f373a724ec6566ea2eb824ee13b22711","bundle_entry_key":"kruskal-sublevel-intrinsic-diameter-lower-transfer","bundle_id":"population.w344-open-problem-c0087-checked-partials-20261010","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"w344/bundle_007.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:probability.minimum_spanning_trees:kruskal-sublevel-intrinsic-diameter-lower-transfer","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Open Problem 16.3 in the same cached source asks for MST diameter of order V^(1/3) on high-dimensional tori and the hypercube. The prior proposed reduction lacked complete geometric hypotheses. We prove only its deterministic lower-bound step.

Let G be a finite connected graph with distinct edge weights, T its MST, and G_p the graph of edges with weight <=p. Kruskal's algorithm has, by level p, selected a spanning tree inside every component C of G_p. These selected edges remain in T. Hence the unique T path between any u,v in C lies inside C, and its length is at least their shortest-path distance in C. Consequently max_C diam_intrinsic(C)<=diam(T). Any probabilistic lower bound for the left side transfers directly to the MST diameter without a universality assumption.

The reverse inequality fails: on K_n give the edges of a Hamilton path the n-1 smallest weights and every other edge larger distinct weights. Kruskal selects exactly that path, so diam(T)=n-1. At a threshold including every edge, G_p=K_n has intrinsic diameter 1. Thus intrinsic cluster-diameter control at one level gives no MST upper bound. This does not refute high-dimensional scaling. The script checks all 38 connected labeled four-vertex graphs at every selected threshold and exact K_4,K_8,K_16 path examples. Full torus/hypercube bounds remain unresolved in this packet.","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:ded8fddb33a606a358455f809c33b51023b9de4319e7cfbdbc3fdfbe6e6a4a4b","revision_digest":"sha256:ff2c306f2aacf69a54d5699409acc2e8b7a73e6493942f300116f1003df99af2","source_id":"owner-w344-open-problems-20261010:kruskal-sublevel-intrinsic-diameter-lower-transfer","source_revision":"2026-10-10 worker result; proof; independent audit pending w346; novelty unverified","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

body:
[Lemma lem:Description of arXiv:2607.14062, 'Gilbert's disc model conditioned on the square lattice'] Let $k\geq1$ be such that $s_{n_k}= s_{n_k+2}= \hspace{.1cm}\rightarrow\hspace{.1cm}$ and $s_{n_k+1}= s_{n_k+3} = \hspace{.1cm}\uparrow\hspace{.1cm}$. Then, 
\begin{itemize}
    \item if $n_{k+1} - n_k = 2l$ with $l\geq1$, then between the points $u_{n_{k}+1}$ and $u_{n_{k+1}+1}$, the path alternates between the patterns  $(\hspace{.1cm}\rightarrow\hspace{.1cm},\hspace{.1cm}\uparrow\hspace{.1cm})$ and $(\hspace{.1cm}\rightarrow\hspace{.1cm},\hspace{.1cm}\downarrow\hspace{.1cm})$, i.e.,
    $$\forall m\in\{2,\ldots, 2l+1\}, \quad s_{n_k + m} =
    \begin{cases}
        \hspace{.1cm}\rightarrow\hspace{.1cm} & \text{if } m = 0 \mod 2,\\
        \hspace{.1cm}\downarrow\hspace{.1cm} & \text{if } m = 1 \mod 4,\\
        \hspace{.1cm}\uparrow\hspace{.1cm} & \text{if } m =3 \mod 4.
    \end{cases}
    $$
    \item if $n_{k+1} - n_k = 2l+1$ with $l\geq1$, then between the points $u_{n_{k}+1}$ and $u_{n_{k+1}+2}$, the paths alternates between the patterns $(\hspace{.1cm}\rightarrow\hspace{.1cm},\hspace{.1cm}\uparrow\hspace{.1cm})$ and $(\hspace{.1cm}\leftarrow\hspace{.1cm},\hspace{.1cm}\uparrow\hspace{.1cm})$, i.e., 
    $$\forall m\in\{2,\ldots,2l+2\}, \quad s_{n_k + m} = 
    \begin{cases}
        \hspace{.1cm}\uparrow\hspace{.1cm} & \text{if } m  = 1 \mod 2,\\
        \hspace{.1cm}\leftarrow\hspace{.1cm} & \text{if } m  = 0 \mod 4,\\
        \hspace{.1cm}\rightarrow\hspace{.1cm} & \text{if } m  = 2 \mod 4.
    \end{cases}
    $$
\end{itemize}
	In particular, for any $k \geq 1$,
	$$s_{n_{k+1}} = \left\{
	\begin{array}{ll}
		\hspace{.1cm}\rightarrow\hspace{.1cm} &\mbox{ if } n_{k+1} - n_k \mbox{ is even},\\
		\hspace{.1cm}\uparrow\hspace{.1cm} &\mbox{ if } n_{k+1} - n_k \mbox{ is odd}.  
	\end{array}
	\right.$$

[Lem 2 of arXiv:2604.25964, 'Strong convergence rate of Euler-Maruyama approximations in temporal-spatial H\"older-norm'] (A Lyapunov estimate) 
 Let $V\in C^{1,2}([0, T]\times \mathbb{R}, [0, \infty)), \alpha \in L([0, T], [0, \infty))$ with $\int_0^T \alpha (t) dt<\infty,$ let $\tau: \Omega\to [0, T]$ be a stopping time, let the coefficients $\mu, \sigma, \gamma$ be continuous functions and let $X: [0, T] \times \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$ be the c\`adl\`ag and adapted  solution process to the equation \eqref{eqn1} with sample paths satisfying
\begin{align}

        & \dfrac{\partial V}{\partial t} (t \land \tau, X_{t \land \tau}) + \dfrac{\partial V}{\partial x} (t \land \tau, X_{t \land \tau}) \mu (X_{t \land \tau})+ \dfrac{1}{2} \dfrac{\partial^2 V}{\partial x^2}(t \land \tau, X_{t \land \tau}) \sigma^2 (X_{t \land \tau}) \notag \\
        & \quad + \int_{\bR_0} \left[ V(t \land \tau, X_{t \land \tau} + \gamma (X_{t \land \tau})z ) - V(t \land \tau, X_{t \land \tau}) - \dfrac{\partial V}{\partial x}(t \land \tau, X_{t \land \tau}) \gamma (X_{t\land \tau}) z \right] \nu (dz) \notag \\
        & \leq \alpha (t \land \tau) V(t \land \tau, X_{t \land \tau}), \quad \mathbb{P} \text{-a.s. for all } t \in [0,T].
    \end{align}
    Then,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}\left[V(\tau, X_{\tau})\right] \leq \exp \left( \int_0^T \alpha(s) ds \right) \mathbb{E} [V(0, X_0)] \in [0,\infty].
\end{align*}

